# Concept Design

Read `docs/foundation/004_concept_design_method.md`, `docs/concepts/concept_template.md`, and `docs/concepts/phase_002/README.md` before proposing or changing concepts.

- Start with an actor need and one primary purpose.
- A concept is an independent unit of functionality, not automatically a class, service, table, endpoint, UI page, repository, Databricks job, or vendor feature.
- Every accepted concept must define: name, purpose, operational principle, state, actions, invariants, ambiguity behavior, and synchronizations.
- Prefer one concept ↔ one primary purpose as a design heuristic.
- If a candidate has unrelated purposes, split/rethink it.
- Compose concepts using explicit synchronizations instead of merging state and responsibilities.
- Keep domain entities separate from concepts when appropriate; a Data Asset can participate in many concepts without becoming one giant concept.
- Do not make vendor-shaped concepts (`DQXConcept`, `CollibraConcept`, `ImmutaConcept`, `UnityCatalogConcept`). Define product functionality first, then evaluate the tool as a realization/provider.
- Do not map concept boundaries to technical architecture during product discovery.
- Test concepts against happy, degraded, missing-evidence, conflicting-evidence, and unauthorized-evidence scenarios.
- Use the Table A + Table B → Table C degradation scenario as a recurring concept test.

## Phase 002 review discipline

- Review concepts in the strategic group order defined in `docs/concepts/phase_002/README.md`.
- Treat every seeded specification as Candidate until explicitly reviewed/accepted.
- Later-group drafts may be refined for consistency, but do not silently promote them while an earlier group is still active.
- Boundary refinement is expected: split, merge, rename, or reject candidates when one-purpose-per-concept analysis warrants it.
- Record meaningful boundary decisions in `docs/decisions/README.md` and update the catalog/glossary together.
